virtual-particles-caed997c.interpretation:
The equation 
\mathbf{F}_{fict} = -m\mathbf{A}_{frame}
 describes the pseudo-force 
\mathbf{F}_{fict}
 experienced by an object of mass 
m
 when observed from a non-inertial reference frame that is accelerating with an acceleration 
\mathbf{A}_{frame}
 relative to an inertial frame. The term 
\mathbf{F}_{fict}
 is a vector representing the pseudo-force. The scalar 
m
 is the inertial mass of the object experiencing this apparent force. The vector 
\mathbf{A}_{frame}
 is the acceleration of the non-inertial reference frame itself. The crucial negative sign indicates that the pseudo-force acts in the direction exactly opposite to the acceleration of the non-inertial frame. For instance, if a bus accelerates forward, passengers feel a pseudo-force pushing them backward, which is explained by this formula.

core-composition-cf491a29.conceptual_definition:
This formula establishes the condition for chemical (beta) equilibrium in a system where neutrons can interconvert with protons and electrons, specifically through processes like neutron decay (
\(n \rightarrow p + e^- + \bar{\nu}_e\)
) and electron capture (
\(p + e^- \rightarrow n + \nu_e\)
). It states that at equilibrium, the chemical potential of a neutron is equal to the sum of the chemical potentials of a proton and an electron.
